(LST-35 — RESOLVED, 2026-08-22, vox #68. On 0.4.9 and earlier the
 EXPRESSION form of `{...}` interpolation had no runtime-tag dispatch, so
 a nested list did not render there and instead printed a live heap
 address that changed between runs. LANGUAGE.md's two sentences
 documenting the limitation are gone in 0.4.10 (LANGUAGE.md:2498 now
 reads "One limitation remains for this stage", where 0.4.9 read "Two").
 #68 fixed the underlying bug: the hole's expression path now dispatches
 on the slot's runtime tag "in every sink — Print, a text initializer,
 buffer set/copy/append, write, filesystem paths, treating clauses and
 function arguments" (CHANGELOG.md #68). This probe tests three of those
 sinks: Print, a text initializer, and a buffer copy.
 This also closes the expression-form sub-case of Discrepancy 7 (tracked
 there as vox candidate #68); the variable-form sub-case was already
 resolved by #44.
 Ran: VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox LST-35.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   [2, 3]
   [2, 3]
   [2, 3]
)
a list called nested is [1, [2, 3], "four"].
print "{element 2 of nested}".
a text called rendered is "{element 2 of nested}".
print rendered.
a buffer called sink is 64 bytes in size.
copy "{element 2 of nested}" to sink.
print sink.
